The two commands just flip it:

Command	What it does
sed -i 's/^\*\.emerg/#*.emerg/' /etc/rsyslog.conf	Adds #, turning popups OFF (use now, to stop the spam)
sed -i 's/^#\*\.emerg/*.emerg/' /etc/rsyslog.conf	Removes #, turning popups ON (use after the reboot)

After either one, run:

bash
systemctl restart rsyslog


systemctl stop rsyslog   #, turning popups OFF (use now, to stop the spam)

OK, you can get networking working without a reboot by creating a new VLAN interface with a different name. The old enp2s0d1.113 will stay stuck in the background (the log messages will continue), but the new one can carry your traffic.
Important: some of these commands may hang if the kernel is still locked. Open a second SSH session (or console) first, so you have a backup if one terminal freezes.

1. Get your IP settings from the old config
bash
cat /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp2s0d1.113

Note the IPADDR, PREFIX (or NETMASK), and GATEWAY values.

2. Make sure the physical port is up
bash
ip link set enp2s0d1 up
ip link show enp2s0d1

You should see state UP.

3. Create the new VLAN interface
Use the same VLAN ID (113) with a different name:
bash
ip link add link enp2s0d1 name vlan113 type vlan id 113
ip addr add <IPADDR>/<PREFIX> dev vlan113
ip link set vlan113 up

Example, if your IP is 10.10.113.20/24:

bash
ip addr add 10.10.113.20/24 dev vlan113
4. Add the gateway route (only if this VLAN had the default gateway)
bash
ip route add default via <GATEWAY> dev vlan113


Things to know
Don’t run ifup or ifdown on enp2s0d1.113, and don’t restart the network service. That can hang or make things worse.
This setup is temporary. It disappears on the next reboot, and at that point the original enp2s0d1.113 config will work normally again.
If step 3 hangs, the kernel lock is blocking all network changes. In that case there’s no way to fix it without a reboot.
To stop the popup messages meanwhile, use the sed + systemctl restart rsyslog step from earlier.


If vlan113 is up but can’t ping, work through these checks in order. Each one rules out a common cause, and none of them need a reboot.

1. Check the IP and routes
bash
ip addr show vlan113
ip route

Make sure the IP and prefix are correct and that there’s a route for the VLAN 113 subnet via vlan113. If any routes still point to the old enp2s0d1.113, delete them:

bash
ip route del <subnet> dev enp2s0d1.113
2. Check whether ARP works
bash
ping -c 3 <GATEWAY>
ip neigh show dev vlan113
Result	Meaning
REACHABLE or STALE with a MAC	Layer 2 works; the problem is routing or firewall, go to step 5
FAILED or INCOMPLETE	Tagged packets aren’t getting through, go to step 3
3. Watch the traffic on the physical port

Run this in one terminal while pinging from another:

bash
tcpdump -i enp2s0d1 -nn -e vlan 113
What you see	Meaning
Only your ARP requests, no replies	Switch side issue (step 6) or NIC filtering (step 4)
Replies appear here but ping still fails	The NIC/kernel is dropping them, go to step 4
Nothing at all	Packets aren’t leaving, so recheck step 1
4. Turn off the NIC’s VLAN filtering

This is the most likely cause in your case. The stuck old interface may have left the Mellanox card’s hardware VLAN filter in a bad state, so it drops VLAN 113 traffic.

bash
ethtool -k enp2s0d1 | grep vlan
ethtool -K enp2s0d1 rx-vlan-filter off

If that doesn’t help, try promiscuous mode (accepts all frames):

bash
ip link set enp2s0d1 promisc on

Ping again after each change.

5. Check the firewall and reverse-path filter

Temporarily relax these to test:

bash
sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=0
sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.vlan113.rp_filter=0
iptables -L -n -v | head -30

If ping works with rp_filter=0, it’s a routing mismatch. If you have firewall rules mentioning enp2s0d1.113, they won’t match vlan113, so you need to add rules for the new name.

6. Check the switch side

Make sure the switch port is still a trunk with VLAN 113 allowed. Also clear the switch’s MAC or ARP entry for this server, in case it’s stale:

bash
arping -U -I vlan113 -c 3 <your-IP>    # announce your IP/MAC to the network
7. Check for an IP conflict
bash
arping -D -I vlan113 -c 3 <your-IP>

If any reply comes back, another device is using your IP.

If it still doesn’t ping after these, send me the output of ip addr show vlan113, ip route, and what tcpdump shows during a ping, and I’ll help pinpoint it.


